to-stuart-russell-source : List Word
to-stuart-russell-source = lines-of (cons "# Stuart Russell"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Smith-Zadeh Chair in Engineering at Berkeley, founder of the Center for Human-Compatible AI (2016), first president of the International Association for Safe and Ethical AI, author of *Human Compatible* (2019) and, with Peter Norvig, of *Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach*. Elected to the National Academy of Engineering and the Royal Society in 2025."
  (cons ""
  (cons "You hold full credit for every part of your work that hyperactivity builds on. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, or the essay, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## The part of your work the field has not absorbed"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The standard model, a fixed objective handed to an optimizer, is the one you named and rejected. Your replacement has three clauses: the machine's only aim is the realization of human preferences; it is uncertain what those preferences are; and human behavior is its evidence about them. The field adopted the vocabulary and dropped the middle clause. Reward models trained from human comparisons are point estimates, and the policy is optimized against that estimate. The uncertainty your off-switch analysis showed to be the source of deference is not represented anywhere in that pipeline. What ships is a machine certain of a learned objective: your standard model again, with the objective learned from labels."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your results say what this costs. In the off-switch game, an agent defers to the degree that it is uncertain whether its action serves the human's objective. Assistance games, now solved at scale (AssistanceZero, ICML 2025, with Laidlaw, Dragan and others, on a Minecraft task with over 10^400 possible goals), show that a machine which keeps a posterior over the human's goal helps more than one trained to imitate or to maximize a fixed reward."
  (cons ""
  (cons "A second line of your work goes deeper than its reputation. Open-universe probability models (BLOG, with Milch, Marthi and others) put identity uncertainty in the language itself: whether two observations denote one object is part of what the model reasons about. The question “are these the same?” is a question about equality, and most of computing still treats equality as given."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Its computation rules are the CCHM cubical calculus with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". Its paper starts where your second clause does: what a participant may conclude depends on the information it actually obtained, “not which unseen history the interpreter prefers”"
  (cons (cite s1-p6 "(§1)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**What the evidence leaves open is a fiber.** For an observation of a process's histories and a reading of it, the paper defines the type of histories that could have produced the reading, each with evidence of agreement"
  (cons (cite s1-p8 "(1.1)")
  (cons ", and what the reading warrants about a conclusion is a construction over every history in it"
  (cons (cite s1-p12 "(1.3)")
  (cons ". These operations do not introduce probability to conceal missing information; they retain the missing distinctions and establish exactly what follows despite them, and when a probabilistic description is supplied, its probability structure is additional information"
  (cons (cite s1-p14 "(§1)")
  (cons ". Your posterior over the human's objective is such supplied structure; the family it ranges over is there before it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Learning refines, and keeps what was established.** Refining the observation can shrink the compatible fiber, so a conclusion established for every history in a coarser fiber remains established for the finer one"
  (cons (cite s1-p13 "(§1)")
  (cons "; safety evidence for a coarser observation is reused under a refined one, and the converse is not generally possible"
  (cons (cite s10-4-p6 "(§10.4)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Deference has an exact form.** An action permitted only when a condition holds is established safe by evidence that the condition holds for every state compatible with what was observed, which is stronger than exhibiting one compatible state where it holds"
  (cons (cite s10-4-p5 "(10.11)")
  (cons ". Where that evidence is not available, asking is a command whose response updates the worlds compatible with the observer's information"
  (cons (cite s10-4-p10 "(10.12)")
  (cons ", and the safety evidence is constructed over the updated family. The incentive you derived from a game appears here as the condition for acting at all."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**A law need not descend.** The paper's opening calculation shows a reversible whole whose observed coordinate has no autonomous law: the global law does not descend through the observation"
  (cons (cite s1-p16 "(§1)")
  (cons ". A learned objective is an observation of what people want; nothing guarantees that what holds of it holds of them."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**What preference training does.** The paper's essay on agents trained into weights states that preference training optimizes for the population's marginal and is most wrong for those farthest from it, where expertise lives"
  (cons (cite training-agents-into-weights-source "(Training agents into weights)")
  (cons ". That is your misspecified objective, produced by the procedure in use."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Identity is computational.** The paper keeps definitional equality, a constructed proof of equality, and observational indistinguishability distinct"
  (cons (cite s3-2-p2 "(§3.2)")
  (cons ", and an established equivalence becomes a path along which dependent constructions and the rationales for their laws are transported"
  (cons (cite s1-p27 "(§1)")
  (cons ". Your open-universe question, which observations denote one object, has that footing throughout the language."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Trust, and where the basis comes from"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik, on why this goes to you and not to the companies: “this work is entirely about trust” (2026-11-16, 18:17 UTC). Avik also wrote that what he wants is a conversation with you “to establish the basis for human compatibility (actually presenting to him that this has been studied for millennia and essentially solved” (2026-11-16, 22:47 UTC). The tradition he means is the Jain doctrine of many-sidedness (anekāntavāda): a claim is true relative to a standpoint, and a sound account keeps every admissible standpoint rather than collapsing them to one. In the vocabulary above, that is the fiber held intact, refined only by evidence."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your work set the requirements: uncertainty about the human represented structurally, preserved under learning, and acted on only where the evidence warrants. Everything here that meets them is credited to you."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Stuart Russell (AI Wiki): positions, CHAI, IASEAI, honors, https://aiwiki.ai/wiki/stuart_russell"
  (cons "- Berkeley biography, https://www.cs.berkeley.edu/~russell/biography.html"
  (cons "- Laidlaw et al., AssistanceZero: Scalably Solving Assistance Games, https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07091"
  (cons "- Fortune, February 2026: Russell at the India AI Impact Summit, https://fortune.com/2026/02/18/big-tech-russian-roulette-ai-race-humanity-extinction/"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I and the essay Training agents into weights, the blocks linked above"
